---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ContentSection from '../components/ContentSection.astro';
import LinkCard from '../components/LinkCard.astro';

const relatedPages = [
  { title: 'Line Movement', href: '/understanding-line-movement', description: 'Why lines change' },
  { title: 'Public vs Sharp', href: '/public-vs-sharp-money', description: 'Who moves markets' },
  { title: 'Betting Strategy', href: '/betting-strategy', description: 'Framework for decisions' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="How Betting Markets React to Major Events"
  description="Understanding how betting markets respond to injuries, news, weather, and other significant events that affect game outcomes."
>
  <Hero
    title="How Markets React to Major Events"
    subtitle="Understanding Market Response to News and Information"
    size="medium"
  />

  <div class="container content-container">
    <article class="page-content">
      <ContentSection>
        <div class="quick-take">
          <h2>Quick Take</h2>
          <p>
            Betting markets react rapidly to significant events: injuries, weather changes, lineup announcements, and breaking news. Understanding how markets process information—and how quickly—helps you evaluate whether acting on news creates opportunity or just chases already-adjusted prices. Modern markets are efficient; by the time most bettors hear news, lines have already moved.
          </p>
        </div>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="How Do Markets Process Information?" id="process">
        <h3>Speed varies by source</h3>
        <p>
          Official announcements (injury reports, starting lineups) move markets instantly. Rumors and speculation move markets more gradually and inconsistently.
        </p>

        <h3>Magnitude depends on impact</h3>
        <p>
          A quarterback injury moves NFL lines more than a backup tight end injury. Market response scales with the news's expected effect on outcomes.
        </p>

        <h3>Algorithms lead, humans follow</h3>
        <p>
          Sportsbooks use automated systems to adjust lines based on news feeds. Human bettors reacting to the same news are typically betting worse numbers.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What This Means for Bettors" id="what-means">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Speed matters:</strong> If you don't have an information edge, you're betting adjusted prices.</li>
          <li><strong>Overreaction creates opportunity:</strong> Sometimes markets overadjust to news, but identifying this requires confident contrary analysis.</li>
          <li><strong>Price the news, don't react emotionally:</strong> Ask "what should the line be now?" not "should I bet because news broke?"</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Key Takeaways" id="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>Markets adjust to news faster than most bettors can react</li>
          <li>Response magnitude scales with news significance</li>
          <li>Overreactions can create opportunities for those with contrary analysis</li>
          <li>Chasing news typically means betting worse numbers</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Related Resources" id="related">
        <div class="related-grid">
          {relatedPages.map((page) => (
            <LinkCard title={page.title} href={page.href} description={page.description} />
          ))}
        </div>
      </ContentSection>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-content { padding: var(--spacing-3xl) 0; }
  .quick-take { background-color: var(--color-surface); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent); padding: var(--spacing-lg); border-radius: 0 var(--border-radius) var(--border-radius) 0; margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xl); }
  .quick-take h2 { font-size: 1rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--color-accent); margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm); }
  .quick-take p { margin: 0; color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .related-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: var(--spacing-md); margin-top: var(--spacing-lg); }
  @media (max-width: 768px) { .related-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
